*Context: *Last week=E2=80=99s Around the Horn vote produced a runaway winn=
er: a
strategic reading of China=E2=80=99s AI Plus initiative (link to *original =
Chinese
<https://substack.com/redirect/8f53e4ef-d4c5-488a-83d1-f394f02acc31?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*),
from Synced (*jiqizhixin*). My readers love a plan. Last month, China=E2=80=
=99s
State Council released implementation guidelines for its AI Plus
initiative, which was initially proposed in a 2024 work report. The *jiqizh=
ixin
*report deems these implementation guidelines as more targeted and
systematic than China=E2=80=99s New Generation AI Plan from 2017.

And you all were right to choose this one: it=E2=80=99s a useful breakdown,=
 and
more importantly, it serves as a good launching pad for me to give some
grouchy comments and hot takes.

*Key Takeaways: The plan positions AI as China=E2=80=99s key growth engine =
for the
future (=E2=80=9Cthe core of new quality productive forces=E2=80=9D, accord=
ing to *
*jiqizhixin=E2=80=99**s reading). In the past, these productivity forces ha=
ve
included emerging industries
<https://substack.com/redirect/5b20f1dc-a180-4f79-88df-6fabbefdf6c2?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
such as nuclear fusion or quantum computing. Here, AI stands above the
rest. These implementation guidelines lay out three-step blueprint:*

   -

   By 2027: achieve over 70% penetration rate of intelligent terminals and
   agents in six key sectors.
   -

   By 2030: achieve over 90% penetration rate of intelligent terminals and
   agents across the entire economy.
   -

   By 2035: AI will usher in an intelligent economy and society. The
*jiqizhixin
   *report interprets this as: =E2=80=9C*AI will be as universally adopted =
as
   electricity and the internet, becoming the =E2=80=98base infrastructure=
=E2=80=99 of
   society.*=E2=80=9D

*Let=E2=80=99s go through some of the planks that stood out to me. First, t=
he six
key sectors targeted for a 70% AI adoption rate are: science and
technology, industry, consumption, social well-being, governance, and
global cooperation.*

   -

   This is such a weird mishmash of industries to highlight as early
   adopters. Global cooperation is: a) not really a sector, b) will see muc=
h
   slower AI diffusion as compared to, say, finance, and c) does not have a=
n
   outsized impact on economic productivity. Industrial devevelopment, the
   second sector listed, basically includes the entire economy, as it inclu=
des
   industry, agriculture, and services. The one that makes sense to me is A=
I
   for science, as a promising application sector where AI could =E2=80=9C*=
transform
   the R&D chain, significantly shortening the distance between the laborat=
ory
   and the market*.=E2=80=9D

*The goals and timelines are unbelievably optimistic, especially 90 percent
adoption rate across the entire economy by 2030! As the article points out,
=E2=80=9C**The 90% figure means that nearly every industry, every organizat=
ion, and
even the majority of individual users will be using smart devices or
agents, moving AI from application-specific landing towards an economy-wide
driver**.=E2=80=9D*

   -

   People often ask me if I think that Chinese policymakers are reading my
   book. It makes me laugh for a number of reasons. First of all, I doubt m=
any
   people have actually read the whole book. So, no, Xi Jinping and his
   advisors are not poring through my duration model of how fast 76 differe=
nt
   countries reached a certain level of computerization. Even my wife skipp=
ed
   that section.
   -

   Second, if Chinese leaders were taking my book seriously, they would
   have picked a very different benchmark. In the section titled, =E2=80=9C=
The
   Decisive Years in the US-China AI Competition,=E2=80=9D here=E2=80=99s w=
hat I wrote, =E2=80=9C*Thus,
   regardless of which arrival date is used, if AI, like previous GPTs,
   requires a prolonged period of gestation, substantial productivity payof=
fs
   should not materialize =C2=ADuntil the 2040s and 2050s*.=E2=80=9D

   <https://substack.com/redirect/316bd41e-14f7-4141-a020-90b3c49a46d9?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Ding,
   Jeffrey. *Technology and the Rise of Great Powers: How Diffusion Shapes
   Economic Competition*. Princeton University Press, 2024.
   -

      Stay with me here as we run the numbers. How long does it usually
      take for a general-purpose technology (GPT), like AI, to make
its mark? The
      first step in this exercise is to establish an =E2=80=9Carrival date=
=E2=80=9D
for the GPT.
      My book uses Jovanovic and Rousseau=E2=80=99s measure=C2=B9 of when a=
 GPT attains
      1% adoption in the median sector. According to this approach, electri=
city
      arrived as a GPT in 1894, and the computer arrived as a GPT in
1971. Based
      on these arrival dates, it took over three decades for both
electrification
      and computerization to reach 70 percent of households in the U.S. (th=
e
      fastest adopting country). **Note: According to Jovanovic and
      Rousseau, it took even longer than three decades for the median
      manufacturing sector to achieve 70 percent adoption of electricity (a=
s a
      share of total horsepower)*
      -

      In *Technology and the Rise of Great Powers*, I estimate AI=E2=80=99s=
 arrival
      as a potential GPT to sometime in the mid-to-late 2010s. This is
based on a
      2018 census survey metric, with 2.75 percent of firms in the
median sector
      reporting the use of AI technologies. If AI follows the path of
electricity
      and the computer, we will not even get close to 70 percent
adoption across
      the economy =E2=80=94 let alone 90 percent! =E2=80=94 until after 204=
0.

   -

   In sum, to my friends who are so afraid of information hazards that they
   forget how to produce information in the first place: Don=E2=80=99t worr=
y! I have
   not written a playbook for Chinese leaders to adopt. They probably aren=
=E2=80=99t
   reading it; and if they are, they=E2=80=99re certainly not reading it cl=
osely.

*Okay, I should say something nice about these AI Plus implementation
guidelines, which should be complimented for taking diffusion seriously. I
did appreciate the sections on open-source communities: =E2=80=9C**Establis=
h
evaluation and incentive mechanisms for open-source contributions, and
encourage universities to recognize such contributions in student-credit
and faculty-assessment systems**=E2=80=9D *(translation from the superb *Ge=
opolitechs
newsletter
<https://substack.com/redirect/88919d95-e99e-4e14-8576-7176a53361f9?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
).

   -

   Two things can be true at the same time about China=E2=80=99s open-sourc=
e AI
   ecosystem. First, Chinese labs are dominant in producing top open-source=
 AI
   models. It was notable to see *jiqizhixin* cite Interconnects, Design
   Arena, and Hugging Face, where Chinese models top leaderboards. Second,
   these student-credit and faculty-assessment planks are designed to addre=
ss
   low activity and weak participation levels in China=E2=80=99s overall op=
en-source
   software community.

*One last small snack for thought. This interpretation of the AI Plus
implementation guidelines mentioned one Chinese AI regulation that I had
overlooked: Measures for the Security Management of Facial Recognition
Technology Applications
<https://substack.com/redirect/2bef3b47-f1ea-419c-a4aa-2f4aefabb205?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>,
effective June 2025. *

   -

   From the *jiqizhixin *article: =E2=80=9C*The core of these measures is t=
o
   strictly regulate the use of facial recognition=E2=80=A6Data must be sto=
red locally
   and retained for a limited period, and important applications must be
   registered. Facial recognition must not be mandatory as the sole method =
for
   identity verification, and alternative methods must be provided. Data
   collection in public places must be reasonable and legal, and the
   deployment of equipment in private spaces is prohibited*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   My question is why was there no English-language analysis of these
   regulations (including from ChinAI)? I=E2=80=99ve always thought this wo=
uld be
   fascinating project for an enterprising researcher: when and why do
   authoritarian countries curtail the expansion of digital surveillance?
   Another example: we had all the coverage about the dystopian ramificatio=
ns
   of China=E2=80=99s Covid-era health QR codes. Did anyone cover the rollb=
ack?

